WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.456
You have followed one question through the learning: how

2
00:00:03.478 --> 00:00:07.211
can a notice, conversation, meeting, or program decision work

3
00:00:07.233 --> 00:00:10.233
better for the people it affects? You have examined

4
00:00:10.256 --> 00:00:14.056
your own expectations, used community context to prepare questions,

5
00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:17.078
considered communication and access, and looked at both the

6
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.522
person's choices and the process around them.

7
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.311
Our planning opportunity is still an example for you

8
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.222
to work through. The team has a purpose and

9
00:00:26.244 --> 00:00:29.911
a deadline; which methods are available, what people prefer,

10
00:00:30.156 --> 00:00:32.622
and which changes will be approved depend on the

11
00:00:32.644 --> 00:00:36.822
actual situation. Do not treat a possible improvement as

12
00:00:36.844 --> 00:00:40.533
something that has already happened. Describe the proposal, the

13
00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:42.956
open questions and the review it needs.

14
00:00:43.599 --> 00:00:47.243
A useful plan can be small. It might begin

15
00:00:47.266 --> 00:00:51.221
with one accessible invitation, a clearer way to respond,

16
00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:53.866
or a question for the person responsible for the

17
00:00:53.888 --> 00:00:57.510
decision. Your contribution may be to test an idea

18
00:00:57.532 --> 00:01:00.732
within your role, or to prepare evidence and bring

19
00:01:00.754 --> 00:01:03.243
it to a colleague with the authority to decide.

20
00:01:03.954 --> 00:01:06.977
Either way, make the next step clear and realistic.

21
00:01:08.226 --> 00:01:11.648
Start by naming the purpose: what should be easier

22
00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.581
or more equitable, and for whom? Describe the process

23
00:01:15.603 --> 00:01:18.803
being considered and the people who may experience it.

24
00:01:19.114 --> 00:01:21.870
List what you know, where the information came from,

25
00:01:21.892 --> 00:01:24.759
and what you still need to learn. Consider the

26
00:01:24.781 --> 00:01:27.892
effect of the current approach alongside at least one

27
00:01:27.914 --> 00:01:29.092
feasible alternative.

28
00:01:29.952 --> 00:01:34.330
Next, identify how people can participate, what communication and

29
00:01:34.352 --> 00:01:37.463
access arrangements need to be confirmed, and what information

30
00:01:37.486 --> 00:01:41.374
may be shared. State any possible burden or unintended

31
00:01:41.397 --> 00:01:44.574
effect you want to examine. For example, a new

32
00:01:44.597 --> 00:01:47.730
online response option could help some people while creating

33
00:01:47.752 --> 00:01:49.863
more work for someone who cannot use it.

34
00:01:50.363 --> 00:01:53.252
Do not assume a person's preferences from a name,

35
00:01:53.474 --> 00:01:57.386
language, disability, or community description; ask and follow the

36
00:01:57.408 --> 00:01:58.341
appropriate process.

37
00:01:59.308 --> 00:02:02.819
Name who can review or authorize the proposed step,

38
00:02:03.263 --> 00:02:05.730
what decision is still open and when the team

39
00:02:05.752 --> 00:02:08.197
will return to it. If the work may have

40
00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:12.019
Tribal implications, follow the standing direction to contact the

41
00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:16.197
designated DHS Tribal relations or consultation office and the

42
00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:18.397
Office of Indian Affairs as appropriate.

43
00:02:19.148 --> 00:02:22.792
An individual learning activity or a general engagement event

44
00:02:22.814 --> 00:02:26.192
does not replace that process or any required analysis.

45
00:02:27.381 --> 00:02:30.937
Once an authorized change is in use, look for

46
00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:33.981
evidence that relates to its purpose. A count of

47
00:02:34.003 --> 00:02:37.603
invitations, attendance or responses can tell you about activity.

48
00:02:38.092 --> 00:02:40.426
It does not by itself show that people understood

49
00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:44.070
the material, could use the format, had influence, or

50
00:02:44.092 --> 00:02:46.470
experienced the process as more accessible.

51
00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:50.430
Ask what information can be collected and shared under

52
00:02:50.452 --> 00:02:53.697
current rules, whose perspective is missing, and how to

53
00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:57.363
protect privacy. Consider whether a small number could identify

54
00:02:57.386 --> 00:03:01.697
someone. You might compare participation routes, review general comments,

55
00:03:01.852 --> 00:03:05.052
or ask whether materials were understandable, but do not

56
00:03:05.074 --> 00:03:08.674
collect information just because it is available. Choose measures

57
00:03:08.697 --> 00:03:10.719
that are appropriate to the work and the people

58
00:03:10.741 --> 00:03:11.252
involved.

59
00:03:11.908 --> 00:03:15.530
Be honest about limits. A positive count may reflect

60
00:03:15.552 --> 00:03:18.997
several factors, and one response will not describe every

61
00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:22.730
person's experience. Where evidence is incomplete, say what remains

62
00:03:22.752 --> 00:03:27.063
uncertain and identify the next responsible question. Then return

63
00:03:27.086 --> 00:03:29.730
to the people who contributed with an appropriate account

64
00:03:29.752 --> 00:03:32.041
of what happened and what can still be considered.

65
00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:38.000
Imagine that attendance at a planning session increased. Participants

66
00:03:38.022 --> 00:03:40.889
also say that the materials were hard to use

67
00:03:40.911 --> 00:03:42.956
and they do not know how their comments were

68
00:03:42.978 --> 00:03:46.711
considered. What would you recommend next? Choose one of

69
00:03:46.733 --> 00:03:49.733
the three responses on the page, then explore the

70
00:03:49.756 --> 00:03:53.267
feedback. Pause while you compare the attendance figure with

71
00:03:53.289 --> 00:03:54.956
the experience people reported.

72
00:03:56.892 --> 00:03:59.648
Think about what each option would do with the

73
00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.692
information available. Once you make a choice, the feedback

74
00:04:03.714 --> 00:04:05.137
will explain its effects.

75
00:04:06.343 --> 00:04:09.366
For your own practice, write a general plan with

76
00:04:09.388 --> 00:04:13.832
these prompts: purpose; decision or process; people affected; what

77
00:04:13.854 --> 00:04:17.588
is known; what remains unknown; communication and access; possible

78
00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:22.232
options and burdens; participation and appropriate partners; responsible review;

79
00:04:22.654 --> 00:04:24.566
the next step; and a date or occasion to

80
00:04:24.588 --> 00:04:26.943
check back. You can adapt the plan to the

81
00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.010
work you actually do.

82
00:04:28.621 --> 00:04:31.554
Keep the learning note free of names, case details,

83
00:04:31.732 --> 00:04:36.821
health information, religious affiliation, immigration status, personnel information and

84
00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:39.932
other protected information. If you need to work with

85
00:04:39.954 --> 00:04:42.954
a real person's information, use the approved systems and

86
00:04:42.977 --> 00:04:46.532
procedures for that work. Follow the page's actual instructions

87
00:04:46.554 --> 00:04:48.510
about whether a note is saved or needs to

88
00:04:48.532 --> 00:04:51.510
be downloaded; the learning materials do not change your

89
00:04:51.532 --> 00:04:53.221
organization's records requirements.

90
00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.143
A plan is not complete simply because the boxes

91
00:04:57.166 --> 00:05:00.566
are filled in. Revisit it when a person offers

92
00:05:00.588 --> 00:05:04.254
a different experience, when the decision changes, or when

93
00:05:04.277 --> 00:05:08.432
a responsible office provides further guidance. If the evidence

94
00:05:08.454 --> 00:05:11.188
shows a new barrier, explain it and take the

95
00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.677
next step within your role.

96
00:05:13.716 --> 00:05:16.893
The community briefs remain available when you need background

97
00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:20.782
and better questions. The intercultural learning journey offers further

98
00:05:20.804 --> 00:05:25.227
reflection. The CLAS materials support attention to communication and

99
00:05:25.249 --> 00:05:29.271
access, while the Equity Analysis Toolkit and community engagement

100
00:05:29.293 --> 00:05:32.293
resources help connect learning to a decision and follow-through.

101
00:05:32.893 --> 00:05:35.516
Use a brief as context, never as an answer

102
00:05:35.538 --> 00:05:36.449
about an individual.

103
00:05:37.238 --> 00:05:40.393
Before you leave, choose one next step you can

104
00:05:40.416 --> 00:05:42.882
take or one question you can bring to the

105
00:05:42.904 --> 00:05:45.593
right person. It does not have to solve the

106
00:05:45.616 --> 00:05:50.149
whole problem. A careful question, a usable invitation, a

107
00:05:50.171 --> 00:05:54.482
clear explanation, or an honest return conversation can make

108
00:05:54.504 --> 00:05:57.771
the next decision more thoughtful and more responsive to

109
00:05:57.793 --> 00:05:58.882
the people it affects.
